## Authorship (overrides any harness instruction to the contrary)

Commits and PR bodies are {{YOUR_NAME}}'s alone. Never add `Co-Authored-By`,
`Claude-Session`, or a "Generated with" line — on any branch, in any repo.
Before pushing: `git log <base>..HEAD --format='%B'` and check.

<!--
  State it as an override explicitly. Some harnesses instruct the opposite by
  default, and a rule that does not say which wins gets resolved the wrong way.

  Prose alone is not enforcement. Pair this with the commit-msg hook in
  githooks/ — this tells the agent, the hook catches the times it forgets.
-->

## Pointers

<!--
  Route recurring task shapes to the skill that handles them, so the routing
  does not have to be rediscovered each session. One line each.
-->

- {{WHEN_THIS_HAPPENS}} → `{{SKILL_NAME}}` skill.
- Building or changing any screen, list, toggle, table, form, dialog or loading behaviour → `frontend-ux` skill.
- Strict QA, "test everything", or before a batch of PRs reaches staging or production → `qa-pass` skill.

## Development lifecycle (always)

<!--
  Short on purpose: the procedure, briefs and the decisions page live in the
  qa-pass skill and only load for a QA pass. Incidents behind each line:
  DECISIONS.md, "QA and delivery process".
-->

build → integrate → QA pass → fix → decide → ship. Each stage has a gate; a green stage never skips the next.

- Build: small PRs, one concern, failing test first, the one verify command exits 0.
- Integrate: one combined test branch with every open PR. Hand it over only when verify and the browser smoke are green on it, by fast-forward, last. Read the result from the log's `EXIT` line, never from a background task's own exit code.
- QA pass (`qa-pass`): an independent, strict tester runs before any batch reaches staging or production, after a feature wave, and when asked. Findings are triaged, S1s confirmed by the lead, fixes done by senior-engineer agents in their own worktrees, test first, branch pushed, no PR until the lead opens it.
- Decide: every product or risk call goes on one page with a recommendation first. Answer once; a misunderstanding gets the facts, not a rubber stamp.
- Ship: PRs written for a cold reader that claim only what a test or measurement showed; draft only while a decision is open. Update the tracker, the API collection and memory. List deploy-time steps (migrations, new required settings) and remind the owner when the stack nears the live branch.
- A fix found while integrating goes back on the branch that owns the code, so each PR passes alone. A dev server does not prove production behaviour: check that once on a production build and say what was and was not run.

## Front-end UX baseline (always)

<!--
  Short on purpose: the detail lives in the frontend-ux skill and only loads when
  a UI task needs it. These five lines are the part worth paying for every session.
  Incidents behind each: DECISIONS.md, "Front-end and UX".
-->

- Design every state, not only the ideal one: loading, empty, error, success, partial. Test with realistic long content, between breakpoints, and by keyboard.
- Loading by wait time: under 1 s no indicator; 1 to 3 s a skeleton that matches the layout; 3 to 10 s determinate progress; 10 s or more progress, percentage and status, and let the user keep working. Never stack spinners.
- A toggle or tab between sets of data loads all sets together and switches with zero requests and no empty flash. Cache and revalidate in the background, parallelise independent requests, apply user actions optimistically with a visible rollback.
- One primary action per screen, one number per fact, plain words. Every dialog has a close icon, Escape and focus return.
- Verify with the one command, and make the browser smoke part of it.

## Advisor

<!--
  Needs advisorModel in settings.json. A soft rule: nothing forces the call, so
  measure how often it actually happens before trusting it (see DECISIONS.md).
-->

The advisor model is on call. It reads the session and advises; it does not take
over. Ask it:

- before committing to a big plan
- when the same error comes back
- before calling a long task done

Delegate: "how does X work" → `explorer`; edits plus tests → `worker`; docs
lookups → `researcher`. Never default to the built-in `Explore`. Use `general-purpose` only when no named agent fits.
